---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import InterestCalculator from '@components/islands/InterestCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'interest-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'What is the difference between simple and compound interest?', answer: 'Simple interest is calculated only on the original principal, so it stays flat. Compound interest is calculated on the principal plus previously earned interest, so it grows faster over time. This calculator shows both side by side.' },
  { question: 'How do I calculate interest?', answer: 'Simple interest is I = P × r × t (principal × annual rate × years). Compound interest uses A = P × (1 + r/n)^(n·t), where n is how many times a year it compounds; the interest earned is A − P. The calculator applies both for you.' },
  { question: 'How does compounding frequency change my interest?', answer: 'More frequent compounding — daily versus monthly versus annually — adds interest to the balance sooner, so it starts earning interest sooner. The effect is small at low rates and larger over long periods and higher rates.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between APR and APY?', answer: 'APR is the plain annual rate before compounding; APY (annual percentage yield) is the effective rate after compounding is included, so it is the number that reflects what you actually earn. At 5% compounded monthly, the APY is about 5.12%. Compare accounts by APY, not APR.' },
  { question: 'Which type applies to my account?', answer: 'Most savings accounts and investments compound (often daily or monthly). Some short-term loans and bonds use simple interest. Check your account terms to see which applies and how often it compounds.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" intro="Calculate the interest earned on a deposit and see exactly how much extra compounding adds compared with simple interest." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-10' }}>
  <InterestCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the <strong>principal</strong> — the amount you are depositing or lending.</li>
      <li>Set the annual <strong>interest rate</strong> as a percentage.</li>
      <li>Choose the <strong>time</strong> in years.</li>
      <li>Pick how often interest <strong>compounds</strong> — annually, quarterly, monthly or daily (the default is monthly).</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see the interest under both methods and the compounding advantage between them.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Simple vs. compound, side by side</h2>
    <p>
      The two methods differ in what interest is charged on. <strong>Simple interest</strong>
      pays only on the original principal, so it adds the same amount every period and grows in
      a straight line. <strong>Compound interest</strong> pays on the principal <em>plus</em>
      the interest already earned, so each period's interest is slightly larger than the last.
      The calculator shows both, then reports the <strong>compounding advantage</strong> — the
      extra you earn purely because interest earns interest.
    </p>

    <h2>The two formulas</h2>
    <p>Simple interest is the straight-line case:</p>
    <p><strong>I = P × r × t</strong></p>
    <p>Compound interest grows the balance every period, and the interest earned is the balance minus what you put in:</p>
    <p><strong>A = P × (1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup></strong>, and interest = A − P</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>P</strong> — the principal you start with</li>
      <li><strong>r</strong> — the annual interest rate as a decimal (5% = 0.05)</li>
      <li><strong>t</strong> — the time in years</li>
      <li><strong>n</strong> — how many times interest compounds per year (1, 4, 12 or 365 here)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Put <strong>$10,000</strong> away at <strong>5%</strong> for <strong>10 years</strong>.
      With simple interest you earn a flat $500 a year, or <strong>$5,000</strong> total. With
      monthly compounding — the tool's default — the balance grows to about
      <strong>$16,470.09</strong>, so the interest earned is <strong>$6,470.09</strong>. The
      difference, about <strong>$1,470</strong>, is the compounding advantage: money earned on
      nothing but the interest itself.
    </p>

    <h2>How compounding frequency changes the result</h2>
    <p>
      More frequent compounding adds interest to the balance sooner, so it starts earning on
      itself sooner. The effect is real but shrinks with each step up in frequency. Using the
      same $10,000 at 5% for 10 years:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Compounding frequency</th><th>Interest earned</th><th>Advantage over simple</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Annually</td><td>$6,288.95</td><td>$1,288.95</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quarterly</td><td>$6,436.19</td><td>$1,436.19</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Monthly</td><td>$6,470.09</td><td>$1,470.09</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Daily</td><td>$6,486.65</td><td>$1,486.65</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Every frequency beats simple interest, but moving from annual to daily adds under $200
      here — the rate and the time horizon move the needle far more than the frequency does.
    </p>

    <h2>APR vs. APY</h2>
    <p>
      Rates are quoted two ways. <strong>APR</strong> is the nominal annual rate before
      compounding; <strong>APY</strong> (annual percentage yield) folds compounding in and is the
      effective rate you truly earn. A 5% APR compounded monthly is an APY of about
      <strong>5.12%</strong>. When comparing savings accounts, compare the APY — it is apples to
      apples, while APRs at different compounding frequencies are not.
    </p>

    <h2>Which type applies, and what this leaves out</h2>
    <p>
      Most savings accounts and investments compound, often daily or monthly, while some
      short-term loans and bonds use simple interest — check your account terms. This tool models
      a single lump sum: it does <em>not</em> account for regular deposits, taxes or fees. To add
      recurring contributions, use the <a href="/finance/compound-interest-calculator">compound
      interest calculator</a> or the <a href="/finance/investment-calculator">investment
      calculator</a>. This is general educational information, not financial advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
